{
  "dom_id": "step-e16638",
  "name": "Example: Computing the Euclidean Distance",
  "problem": "Suppose $\\mathbf a=[7,\\, 2,\\, 4]^T$ and $\\mathbf b=[2,\\, 3,\\, 1]^T.$ Find the Euclidean distance $d_2$ between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the Euclidean distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_2(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_2\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{2}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{2} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖}_{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{|7-2{|}^{2}+|2-3{|}^{2}+|4-1{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & ≈5.916,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
